# Builds

- The repository is untrusted. Repository code runs only inside Docker builds and game containers. Host preflight reads only the allowed build files and directory entries through `repoFS` (`os.Root`, reject links within paths, verify the opened object matches the one checked, bound sizes). Never read or execute Go source on the host.
- `ParseConfig` and the golem.yaml reader bound the YAML byte size, nodes, depth and keys before typed decoding, and refuse aliases. The deploy config rejects unknown keys. Paths and commands pass `cleanPath`, `cleanURLPath` and `checkWords`. `Dockerfile` re-validates plan values and renders user commands only through `execForm` (JSON); update the goldens with `go test ./internal/build -run Dockerfile -update`. Repository-derived values in `*UserError` go through `safePath` or `logsafe.String`.
- `server.module` (`Plan.Module`, relative to the game root, default `.`) is the directory of `go.mod`: `readGoMod`, server package detection and the generated `go list`/`go build` step use it; `golem-bake`, the web build, `web.dist`, `generatedCommitted` and the fingerprint stay at the game root. The default must render the Dockerfile byte for byte as before (goldens).
- `lfs.include` (`Config.LFS.Include`, `cleanLFSInclude`) is consumed by `internal/git`, not by builds: `LFSInclude(dir)` reads the checkout's `golem-deploy.yaml` through `repoFS` and `ParseConfig` and returns only `*UserError`s; the daemon injects it as `git.Options.LFSInclude` (git cannot import build). Patterns allow `*` but never a comma, white space or a leading `-`, since they are joined into one `--include=` argument.
- `Inspect` builds the preflight plan (engine version, Go toolchain, npm and bake modes, server package selection, and with `BakeCheck` the schema fingerprint from the committed `golem_helpers.go` of the `go-server` output directory: `committedFingerprint` in `fingerprint.go`, through `repoFS`, size-capped, exactly one `const SchemaFingerprint = "<64 hex>"` line; anything unexpected is "" and never fails the build; stored by `FinishBuild` and replaced by the game-reported value on ready). The generated Dockerfile verifies a `main` package and executable artifact. Custom Dockerfiles require the project admin opt-in and cannot select a frontend. The trusted opt-in grants access to the shared builder and other projects’ cache mounts.
- `Service.Run` optionally pulls, requires idle status and verifies the actual Git HEAD. It acquires the daemon-wide slot and uses a single build timeout for preflight, add-on staging, Docker, verification and success persistence. Cancel/timeout terminates and waits for the complete Docker CLI tree before releasing the slot. `Watch` releases the admission reservation and reconciles builds whose job did not finish writing their result. Join build watchers in the daemon shutdown drain before closing the store.
- `BuildSpec.Args` owns the build argv. Use one tag per build, the built-in frontend, the dedicated `BuildConfigDir`, and the fixed build environment. Pass no build args, secrets, SSH agent or entitlements. The daemon writes the Dockerfile and its .dockerignore outside the checkout with mandatory excludes. Verify the iid/metadata digest as a full id with the unique tag and exact reserved labels (`BuiltImage` with `matchBuildLabels`, also used by `BuildImage`).
- Send build output through `logsafe.Writer` with host-path redactions and the rendered-line cap. Job errors have fixed safe messages. Internal build errors and Docker inspect stderr are stripped of raw details at the sinks.
- Build retention and project deletion page through the entire history. `DeleteBuildUnused` and `DeleteProjectUnused` guard database references transactionally. Check container use first, including stopped containers, and never force image removal. Commit row deletion before checkout/image cleanup. The export intent and image cleanup queue in SQLite support retries after failed builds, deletes and daemon restarts. Failed exports are removed only by their own tag after matching ownership labels.
- Static add-on validation, serialization with upload/delete and budget rules are in the static add-ons rule.
- `POST /projects/{id}/builds` holds `projectState.mu`, checks checkout state (or `pull`), reserves a place, creates the build, starts the job in `build.JobPool` and registers `Watch`. An admin PATCH controls `allow_custom_dockerfile`. Routes and access levels live in the route table.
- `testdata/game` is a WebTransport-primary game under the deploy contract (like the reference game), so it can run as a server; keep it that way.
- Verify with `go test ./internal/build/... ./internal/docker/... ./internal/httpapi/... ./internal/store/...`; with Docker `GOLEM_DEPLOY_DOCKER_TESTS=1 go test -p 1 ./internal/build/... ./internal/httpapi/...` (builds and runs `testdata/game`, needs network; exec-form round trip; custom Dockerfile context/entitlement/label checks; `TestIntegrationBuildToServer` in `internal/httpapi/build_integration_test.go`: build -> server start -> ready -> runtime -> realtime config). Symlink tests skip on Windows without symlink privileges; run them on Linux.
